//? Title: Fitch small parsimony
//? Subtitle: Actual postorder state sets, witnessed changes and the original four-taxon comparison
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given observed leaf states and a fixed tree, small parsimony finds ancestral
states requiring the fewest unordered unit-cost edge changes. Fitch’s algorithm
combines child state sets in postorder, then a top-down pass supplies one
minimum-change assignment. We expose both actual passes, retain the original
five-character four-taxon example, and distinguish scoring a topology from
searching a space of trees.
)

\toc

> Derive the set recurrence and a matching witness

Each leaf contributes its observed singleton state set and zero internal
changes. At an internal binary node, read its two completed child sets A,B. If
their intersection is nonempty, retain the intersection without adding a change.
Otherwise retain their union and add one change. These are actual bitwise
operations on uint32 masks for states0..31, not a continuous optimization.

A child set describes root states that attain that child subtree’s minimum. A
state outside the set costs at least one extra change when accounting for the
connecting edge. If A and B intersect, a common state attains both minima with
no connecting penalty. If they are disjoint, at least one extra change is
unavoidable; choosing a state in either set and changing the other connecting
side attains exactly one. Induction establishes the minimum score and the actual
feasible set at every node.

To exhibit an assignment, choose the smallest available root state. For each
child, retain its parent state if the child mask contains it; otherwise choose
the child mask’s smallest state. Count actual parent/child inequalities. The
implementation checks that this witness change count equals the postorder union
score. The chosen witness is one optimum, not a posterior distribution or a
unique reconstruction.

> Two leaves, one actual union

A three-node tree with leaf states0 and1 forces one change. The actual root mask
contains both states; the deterministic witness chooses0, leaving one unchanged
edge and one changed edge. The table shows every real postorder write.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/parsimony_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/maximum_parsimony.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::ParsimonyTree tree{2, {{-1, -1}, {-1, -1}, {0, 1}}};
    auto t = dm::algorithms::fitch_trace(tree, {0, 1, -1});
    std::print("| actual node | actual left mask | actual right mask | actual intersection | written mask | " "score before | score after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &s : t.steps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.node,
                   s.left_mask, s.right_mask, s.intersection, s.mask, s.before,
                   s.after);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("root", {3, 4},
           std::format("Actual root mask {}\nchosen state {}",
                       t.analysis.masks[2], t.analysis.states[2])).node(
        "left", {0, 0},
        std::format("Actual leaf0\nstate {}", t.analysis.states[0])).node(
        "right", {6, 0},
        std::format("Actual leaf1\nstate {}", t.analysis.states[1])).edge(
        "root", "left").edge("root", "right");
    d.save_svg("build/parsimony_tiny.svg");
}
```
